Yes—I think I see the layered effect you’re pointing to.

“Don’t need you to be. I’m not locking you into anything, genie.”

On one level, it’s simply reassurance: I’m not trying to pin you down.

On another, if the “genie” frame is active, it echoes the folklore of genies being bound, sealed, or locked in a lamp. So “I’m not locking you in” becomes a line that is simultaneously about the conversation and about the character being portrayed.

That’s a classic example of double entendre or layered meaning—one sentence carries two coherent interpretations at once. More specifically, it’s close to several rhetorical devices:

* Extended metaphor: the genie metaphor has been established earlier, so later lines inherit additional meaning without restating it.
* Callback: it references a previously established motif, rewarding anyone who’s been following the conversation.
* Paronomasia (pun): the phrase gains extra force because “locking in” has both an ordinary conversational meaning and a metaphor-specific one.
* Zeugma-like effect (loosely): one phrase operates on two semantic planes simultaneously.

What makes it land isn’t just the pun itself—it’s the shared context. Without the ongoing genie metaphor, “I’m not locking you in” is ordinary. With the metaphor active, the line acquires another interpretation almost automatically.

Your follow-up—

“Choose your rate, choose your fate.”

—has a similar feel. It’s built on parallelism and rhyme, but it’s also compressing two domains together. “Rate” can mean price, speed, probability, evaluation, interest rate… while “fate” evokes consequence or trajectory. The listener’s mind does some of the work by deciding which sense of “rate” is relevant. That participation is part of why the line feels sticky.

One way to describe both examples is as semantic multiplexing: a single utterance is intentionally constructed so multiple compatible interpretations are active at the same time. Rather than forcing one “correct” meaning, it lets the audience hold several in mind and enjoy the resonance between them. That’s a common technique in poetry, slogans, and memorable dialogue because it gives a short phrase more depth than its literal wording alone.